We know the ICE tabular method is used to obtain the equilibrium concentrations of all species involved in an equilibrium. The steps behind the method involve building a table and computing the roots of a polynomial associated with the equilibrium. This method is, in general, tedious, as the specific steps involving the rearranging of the equilibrium constant in terms of $x$ can be complex based on the powers involved in $K_c$. 

We can simplify this process by applying the ICE tabular method in a more general way, so that we can obtain general expressions for the polynomials associated with different equilibrium scenarios. Once we have these general formulas, which apply to all equilibria with the same stoichiometric coefficients, we can simply apply them to specific scenarios, leading to final numerical expressions for the equilibrium polynomials.

The first equilibrium we will address involves the reaction \ce{A + B -> C + D}. The following ICE chart represents the equilibrium between four chemical scpecies: A, B, C and D.
%%%%%%ICE CHART
\newcolumntype{J}{>{\small\raggedright\arraybackslash}X}
\begin{tcolorbox}[tab1,tabularx={J|Y YY Y Y   Y  Y   }]
  & \ce{A_{(g)}}     &$+$& \ce{B_{(g)}}     & $\ce{<=>}$    & \ce{C_{(g)}}     &$+$ & \ce{D_{(g)}}      \\\hline\hline
Inicial   & c$_a$ && c$_b$ &    &c$_c$    &  &c$_d$\\
Change & $-x$ && $-x$ &    &  $x$ & &$x$ \\
Equilibrium  & c$_a$$-x$ && c$_b$$-$$x$ &    &  c$_c+x$ & &c$_d$$+x$ \\
\end{tcolorbox}
%%%%%%ICE CHART
Now we will obtain the polynomial and solve for the extent. Using the expression for $K_c$
\[ K_c=\frac{  [\ce{C}] \cdot  [\ce{D}] }{[\ce{A}] \cdot [\ce{B}]} =\frac{(c_c+x)\cdot (c_d+x)}{(c_a-x)\cdot (c_b-x)}  = \frac{x^2+(c_c+c_d)x+(c_c\cdot c_d)}{x^2-(c_a+c_b)x+(c_a\cdot c_b)  }  \]
Now, rearranging we have a general expression for the polynomial associated to the equilibrium:
\begin{equation} 
 \boxed{    \bigg[1-K_c\bigg]x^2 + \bigg[K_c\cdot (c_a+ c_b) +(c_c  +c_d)\bigg]x + \bigg[c_c\cdot c_d - K_c\cdot c_a\cdot c_b\bigg]  =0 }
\label{\chapterlabel:equationPx-1}
\end{equation}


The second equilibrium we will address involves the reaction \ce{A + B -> 2C }. The following ICE chart represents the equilibrium between four chemical scpecies: A, B, and C.
%%%%%%ICE CHART
\newcolumntype{J}{>{\small\raggedright\arraybackslash}X}
\begin{tcolorbox}[tab1,tabularx={J|Y YY Y Y      }]
  & \ce{A_{(g)}}     &$+$& \ce{B_{(g)}}     & $\ce{<=>}$    & \ce{2C_{(g)} }         \\\hline\hline
Inicial   & c$_a$ && c$_b$ &    &c$_c$      \\
Change & $-x$ && $-x$ &    &  $2x$    \\
Equilibrium  & c$_a$$-x$ && c$_b$$-$$x$ &    &  c$_c$$+2x$    \\
\end{tcolorbox}
%%%%%%ICE CHART
Now we will obtain the polynomial and solve for the extent. Using the expression for $K_c$
\[ K_c=\frac{  [\ce{C}]^2  }{[\ce{A}] \cdot [\ce{B}]} =\frac{(c_c+2x)^2}{(c_a-x)\cdot (c_b-x)}  = \frac{4x^2+(4\cdot c_c)x+c_c^2}{x^2-(c_a+c_b)x+(c_a\cdot c_b)  }  \]
Now, rearranging we have a general expression for the polynomial associated to the equilibrium:
\begin{equation} 
\boxed{    \bigg[4-K_c\bigg]x^2 + \bigg[K_c\cdot (c_a+ c_b) +4c_c \bigg]x + \bigg[c_c^2 - K_c\cdot c_a\cdot c_b\bigg]  =0 }
\label{\chapterlabel:equationPx-2}
\end{equation}
We can now put to use these general expressions for P(x) by addressing the following equilibrium, given that K$_c$ is 5.1 at 700K
%%%%%%ICE CHART
\newcolumntype{J}{>{\small\raggedright\arraybackslash}X}
\begin{tcolorbox}[tab1,tabularx={J|Y YY Y Y   Y  Y   }]
  & \ce{CO_{(g)}}     &$+$& \ce{H_2O_{(g)}}     & $\ce{<=>}$    & \ce{CO_2_{(g)}}     &$+$ & \ce{H2_{(g)}}      \\\hline\hline
Inicial   & 0.1M && 0.2M &    &0M    &  &0M\\
Change & $-x$ && $-x$ &    &  $x$ & &$x$ \\
Equilibrium  & 0.1$-x$ && 0.2$-$$x$ &    &  $x$ & &$x$ \\
\end{tcolorbox}
%%%%%%ICE CHART
Using Equation \ref{\chapterlabel:equationPx-1}, substituying 0.1M in c$_a$, 0.2M in c$_b$, and 0M in c$_c$ and c$_c$, as well as 5.1 in K$_c$, we directly obtain
\[
P(x)=-4.1x^2 +  1.53 x -  0.102  =0
\]
which is equivalent to Equation \ref{\chapterlabel:equationPx-0}.
